The test
['org.apache.commons.csv.CSVFormatTest.testDuplicateHeaderElements()']
failed.

The test looks like: 
    @Test(expected = IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public void testDuplicateHeaderElements() {
        CSVFormat.DEFAULT.withHeader("A", "A").validate();
    }

Current bug report: 
{
    "summary": "CSVFormat constructor should reject a header array with duplicate entries"
    "description": "CSVFormat currently accepts whatever header String[] is provided.  \nIt cannot be used if there are duplicate entries so these should be rejected.\n"
}

Recent bug reports: 
RPT#CSV-112: {
 "summary": "HeaderMap is inconsistent when it is parsed from an input with duplicate columns names"
 "description": "Given a parser format for csv files with a header line: ```\nCSVFormat myFormat = CSVFormat.RFC4180.withDelimiter(\",\").withQuoteChar('\"').withQuotePolicy(Quote.MINIMAL)\n\t\t\t\t.withIgnoreSurroundingSpaces(true).withHeader().withSkipHeaderRecord(true);\n``` And given a file with duplicate header names:\nCol1,Col2,Col2,Col3,Col4  \n1,2,3,4,5  \n4,5,6,7,8 \nThe HeaderMap returned by the parser misses an entry because of the Column name being used as a key, leading to wrong behavior when we rely on it."
}

RPT#CSV-106: {
 "summary": "CSVFormat.format allways append null"
 "description": "When I now call  \nCSVFormat.newFormat(';').withSkipHeaderRecord(true).withHeader(\"H1\",\"H2\").format(\"A\",\"B\")  \nI get the output A;Bnull\nThe expected output would be \nA;B"
}

RPT#CSV-111: {
 "summary": "CSVRecord.toMap() fails if row length shorter than header length"
 "description": "Similar to [](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/CSV-96 \"CSVRecord does not verify that the length of the header mapping matches the number of values\"), if .toMap() is called on a record that has fewer fields than we have header columns we'll get an ArrayOutOfBoundsException. ```\n@Test\npublic void testToMapWhenHeaderTooLong() throws Exception {\n   final CSVParser parser = new CSVParser(\"a,b\", CSVFormat.newBuilder().withHeader(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\").build());\n   final CSVRecord record = parser.iterator().next();\n   record.toMap();\n}\n```"
}
